            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Start with the Conclusion</h2>
<p>'Running' and 'selling' are two completely different optimization logics. Getting impressions and traffic means the creative is 'qualified' in the platform algorithm's view; but converting visitors into buyers is the ultimate goal of advertising. Without distinguishing these two dimensions during review, you'll spend a large budget on creatives that 'look great but don't sell.'</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Metrics to Quickly Identify 'Running' vs. 'Selling' Creatives</h2>
<h3>1. High CTR but Low CVR — Typical 'Running' Characteristic</h3>
<p>A creative with CTR exceeding industry average by 1.5x but landing page CVR below 1% often wins with 'visual hooks' but loses on 'value delivery.' This could be due to overly tempting headlines or unclear product selling points in the first 3 seconds.</p>
<p><strong>Operational experience</strong>: When encountering such creatives, don't rush to pause them. Run A/B tests by placing 'core product selling points' and 'usage scenarios' in the first 5 seconds, and observe if CVR improves. If three consecutive versions fail to improve conversion, the product-creative match may be fundamentally problematic.</p>
<h3>2. CPA on Target but Negative ROI — 'Selling' but 'Not Profitable'</h3>
<p>This is a more insidious problem. A cross-border e-commerce team discovered during review that a skincare creative had CPA controlled under $15, meeting expectations, but user first-order LTV was only $8—each customer acquired resulted in a $7 loss. This creative was 'selling' from a traffic perspective but commercially failed.</p>
<p><strong>Operational experience</strong>: Don't just check CPA during review; establish a 'CPA × 3 ≤ LTV' validation logic. If LTV projection is insufficient, either adjust average order value or refine audience targeting to find high-value users.</p>
<h3>3. High Completion Rate but Low Add-to-Cart Rate — Moved but Not Buying</h3>
<p>Creatives with completion rates exceeding 60% typically indicate good content quality and pacing control. But add-to-cart rates below industry average often mean there's a missing direct call-to-action between 'emotional resonance' and 'purchase impulse.'</p>
<p><strong>Operational experience</strong>: Add limited-time offers, inventory urgency, or gift incentives at the creative's end to transform 'being moved' into 'buying now.' One team found that adding 'extra gift with today's order' text at the end increased add-to-cart rate from 1.2% to 2.8%.</p>
<hr />
<h2>A Practical Creative Review SOP</h2>
<p><strong>Step 1: Classify by 'Running-Selling' Two-Dimensional Grid</strong></p>
<p>Divide your creative pool into four categories: runs well and sells well (scale up), runs well but sells poorly (optimize), runs poorly but sells well (targeting issue), runs and sells poorly (eliminate). Move 80% of budget to the first quadrant.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Find the Real Reason for 'Not Selling'</strong></p>
<p>Not all underperforming creatives should be abandoned. Sometimes it's the landing page that's problematic, sometimes price anchoring isn't set well, sometimes the category itself isn't suitable for short-video channels. Be clear about the distinction to apply the right solution.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Set Dual Thresholds for 'Running-Selling'</strong></p>
<p>Recommended minimum standards: CTR &gt; 0.5x industry average, CVR &gt; 1%, CPA &lt; 1/3 of LTV. Creatives meeting all three thresholds simultaneously deserve sustained scaling.</p>
<hr />
<h2>A Final Real Pattern</h2>
<p>The 'running' lifecycle of creatives is typically 2-4 weeks, while the 'selling' window is even shorter—only 48-72 hours for many categories. Once volume stabilizes, feeling secure and only remembering to review when creatives die often means missing the optimal scaling window.</p>
<p><strong>For creative review, consistency beats perfection.</strong></p>
